    "properAnswer": "Inheritance is a fundamental pillar of OOP that allows a class (subclass) to inherit state (fields) and behavior (methods) from another class (superclass) using the `extends` keyword. It models an 'IS-A' relationship—for instance, `Dog IS-A Animal` or `Developer IS-A Employee`. In Java, inheritance promotes code reuse, enables method overriding, and forms the basis of subtype polymorphism. At the memory level, when a subclass object is instantiated on the Heap, its memory layout includes all fields of its superclass followed by its own subclass fields, and its `this` reference points to the combined memory block. Java strictly enforces single inheritance for classes to prevent the Diamond Problem."
