    "properAnswer": "Method overloading occurs when a class has multiple methods with the exact same name but different parameter signatures—differing by parameter count, data types, or parameter ordering. Overloading is a classic example of Compile-Time Polymorphism (Static Binding). During compilation, the Java compiler uses the argument types passed at the call site to select the specific method version to invoke, binding it into bytecode via `invokevirtual` or `invokestatic`. Note that changing ONLY the return type or `throws` clause is insufficient for overloading and will cause a compiler error."
