    "properAnswer": "Method overriding is a mechanism in Java where a subclass provides its own custom implementation for a method already declared in its superclass. Overriding represents Runtime Polymorphism (Dynamic Binding). The overriding method must have the exact same name, parameter list, and a compatible (covariant) return type. At runtime, the JVM uses Dynamic Method Dispatch via the object's `vtable` (virtual method table) in Metaspace to execute the method corresponding to the actual instance on the Heap, regardless of the reference type. Using the `@Override` annotation is best practice because it instructs the compiler to verify signature compatibility."
