    "properAnswer": "Java does NOT support multiple class inheritance—a class can extend only one superclass (`extends ClassA`). This design choice explicitly prevents the **Diamond Problem**, where ambiguous method implementations arise when two superclasses define the same method. However, Java fully supports multiple inheritance of type through interfaces (`implements InterfaceA, InterfaceB`). Starting in Java 8, interfaces can define `default` methods with implementations; if a class implements two interfaces containing identical default method signatures, the compiler mandates that the class explicitly override the method to resolve ambiguity."
