    "properAnswer": "Declaring a class `final` (`public final class String`) prevents any other class from extending it (`extends`). Its primary uses in enterprise software are:\n1) **Immutability & Security**: Guarantees that class invariants and security checks cannot be bypassed or compromised by malicious subclass overriding (e.g. `java.lang.String`, `java.lang.Integer`).\n2) **Performance Optimization**: Allows the JVM Just-In-Time (JIT) compiler to inline method calls (`invokevirtual` devirtualization), removing virtual method table (`vtable`) lookup overhead.\nAttempting to extend a `final` class results in a compile-time error: **'cannot inherit from final ClassName'**."
