    "properAnswer": "The `finally` block creates a guaranteed execution boundary following a `try` (and optional `catch`) block. It is used to execute critical resource cleanup actions—such as closing file streams, releasing database connections, or unlocking `ReentrantLock` instances—ensuring resources are released even if exceptions occur or explicit `return`, `break`, or `continue` statements execute.\n*Modern Best Practice*: For resources implementing `java.lang.AutoCloseable`, **`try-with-resources` (Java 7+) is strongly preferred over explicit `finally` blocks** because it eliminates verbose boilerplate and handles suppressed exceptions correctly."
