    "properAnswer": "In Java, **initialization blocks** are blocks of code enclosed in braces `{}` inside a class body (outside any method or constructor) that execute during class loading or object instantiation:\n\n1) **Static Initialization Blocks (`static { ... }`)**: Execute **once** when the JVM ClassLoader loads the class. Ideal for complex static field setup or loading native libraries.\n2) **Instance Initialization Blocks (`{ ... }`)**: Execute **every time** a new object instance is instantiated. The compiler copies instance blocks into every constructor right after the `super()` call and before the constructor body runs."
