    "properAnswer": "To iterate through an `ArrayList` using an `Iterator`:\n\n```java\nList<String> list = new ArrayList<>(Arrays.asList(\"A\", \"B\", \"C\"));\nIterator<String> iterator = list.iterator();\n\nwhile (iterator.hasNext()) {\n    String item = iterator.next();\n    if (\"B\".equals(item)) {\n        iterator.remove(); // SAFELY removes element from the list\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**Under the Hood Mechanics & Fail-Fast Behavior**:\n- **`modCount` Verification**: The `Iterator` holds an `expectedModCount` initialized to the `ArrayList`'s structural modification count (`modCount`).\n- **Fail-Fast Exception**: If `list.add()` or `list.remove()` is called directly during iteration, `modCount != expectedModCount`, immediately throwing a `ConcurrentModificationException`."
