    "properAnswer": "You can sort an `ArrayList` in Java using three main techniques:\n\n**1. `Collections.sort()` (Natural Order)**:\nRequires elements to implement `Comparable`:\n```java\nList<String> names = new ArrayList<>(Arrays.asList(\"Charlie\", \"Alice\", \"Bob\"));\nCollections.sort(names); // In-place natural sort: [Alice, Bob, Charlie]\n```\n\n**2. `List.sort()` with Lambda / Comparator (Java 8+)**:\n```java\nnames.sort((a, b) -> a.compareToIgnoreCase(b));\nnames.sort(Comparator.reverseOrder());\n```\n\n**3. Stream API (Immutable / Functional)**:\n```java\nList<String> sortedNames = names.stream()\n    .sorted()\n    .collect(Collectors.toList());\n```\n\n**Under the Hood**: Java's list sorting relies on **Timsort** (hybrid of Merge Sort and Insertion Sort), providing $O(N \\log N)$ time performance and guaranteed sort stability."
