    "properAnswer": "**`HashSet`** is the most widely used implementation of the `Set` interface:\n\n**Under the Hood Architecture**:\n1) **HashMap Backing**: `HashSet` does not implement hash table logic from scratch; it delegates directly to an internal `transient HashMap<E, Object> map`.\n2) **The `PRESENT` Constant**: When you call `hashSet.add(element)`, `HashSet` executes `map.put(element, PRESENT)`, using a static dummy `Object PRESENT = new Object()` as the map value.\n\n**Performance & Best Practices**:\n- **Time Complexity**: **$O(1)$** amortized time for `add()`, `remove()`, and `contains()`.\n- **Equality Requirement**: Objects inserted into a `HashSet` **must correctly override `hashCode()` and `equals()`** to ensure hash collision safety and duplicate detection."
