    "properAnswer": "**Atomic Operations** in Java are indivisible operations that complete fully or not at all, with no possibility of another thread observing an intermediate state:\n\n1) **What is Atomic**: Assigning reference variables or primitive types (except non-volatile 64-bit `long`/`double` on older 32-bit JVMs).\n2) **What is NOT Atomic**: Compound operations like `count++`. It expands to three bytecode instructions (`getfield`, `iadd`, `putfield`), allowing race conditions.\n3) **Atomic Classes**: `java.util.concurrent.atomic` (`AtomicInteger`, `AtomicLong`, `AtomicReference`) uses CPU-level **Compare-And-Swap (CAS)** to achieve lock-free thread-safe updates."
