    "question": "240. What is ForkJoinPool and how does Parallel Streams use it?",
    "summary": "ForkJoinPool is a work-stealing thread pool designed for recursive divide-and-conquer tasks; Parallel Streams utilize the shared ForkJoinPool.commonPool() by default.",
    "explanation": [
      "Work-Stealing Architecture: Each worker thread maintains a double-ended queue (deque); idle threads steal tasks from the tail of busy threads' deques.",
      "Parallel Streams Reliance: Calling stream().parallel() submits tasks to ForkJoinPool.commonPool(), whose parallelism defaults to CPU cores minus 1.",
      "Production Danger: Long-running or blocking I/O tasks inside parallel streams starve the common pool, impacting all parallel streams in the JVM."
    ],
